## (1) English Translation

Wei Wu-hou, when he planned affairs, planned them well, and his ministers could not match him; when he left court he had a look of joy on his face. Wu Ts'i advanced and said: "Have you ever heard the words of King Ch'u Pao of Ch'u, as reported to your attendants?" The Wei Wu-hou said: "What were the words of King Ch'u Pao?" Wu Ts'i answered: "King Ch'u Pao, when he planned affairs, planned them well, and his ministers could not match him; when he left court he had a look of sorrow on his face. Shen Kung Wu Chen advanced and asked, saying: 'You held court in the morning and yet you have a look of sorrow on your face. Why?' The King said: 'I planned affairs and planned them well, and my ministers could not match me; that is why I am sorrowful. In the words of the middle minister of my house it is said: "Among the feudal lords, he who gets a master for himself becomes a king; he who gets a friend becomes a hegemon; he who gets a doubter survives; he who plans for himself and finds no one better than himself perishes." Now, on my own unworthiness, my ministers cannot match me — my state is drawing near to perishing! That is why I am sorrowful.'" King Ch'u Pao was sorrowful, but the Wei Wu-hou was joyful." The Wei Wu-hou, hesitating and looking about, bowed twice and said: "It is Heaven that sends the master to shake out my faults."

## (2) The Moral

The story teaches that a ruler's virtue is not shown by the success of his own plans, but by his ability to surround himself with people superior to himself. Joy at being unmatched by one's ministers is the arrogance of a man who believes he is the best — and such a man has no one to correct him, no one to advise him, and no one to succeed him. Sorrow at being surpassed is the wisdom of a leader who knows that his strength lies in the strength of those around him. The king's own words make the point explicit: a lord who finds a *master* for himself becomes a king; one who finds a *friend* becomes a hegemon; one who finds only a *doubter* survives; one who finds no one better than himself perishes.

## (3) Western Counterparts

Yes. Several concepts have clear counterparts in European thought:

- **The wise ruler and the wise counselor.** The closest analogue is the relationship between Solomon and the elders in the Hebrew Bible, or the ideal of the *philosopher-king* in Plato's *Republic* — a ruler who recognizes his own ignorance and seeks counsel. Also relevant is the Christian tradition of humility before God, in which the wise man acknowledges dependence rather than self-sufficiency.

- **The danger of the self-sufficient ruler.** Machiavelli, in *The Prince*, warns repeatedly against a prince who is loved only for his own virtue and who surrounds himself with flatterers rather than honest counselors. He argues that a prince who is too perfect in his own judgment will be overthrown, because he will have no one to correct him.

- **The value of a good counselor.** Aristotle, in the *Nicomachean Ethics* and the *Politics*, discusses the importance of a ruler having friends and advisors who can speak truth to power, and he contrasts the wise man who knows himself with the arrogant man who does not.

- **The idea of the "doubter" as a survival strategy.** The notion that a leader who keeps a skeptic at his side survives, while one who has no one better than himself perishes, resonates with the Western idea of the *adversary* or *contradictor* — the figure whose role is to challenge the ruler's assumptions.

In short, the story's central insight — that a leader's greatness is measured by the greatness of those around him, and that self-sufficiency in judgment is a danger rather than a virtue — has a long and clear tradition in European political and moral thought. [end of text]


